sections.body:
A golden dataset that gets built once at launch and never touched again slowly stops reflecting reality -- real usage patterns shift, new document categories get added, and failures that show up in production reveal gaps the original sample never covered. The discipline that keeps a golden dataset useful indefinitely is the same one that keeps a codebase's test suite useful: store it as a versioned, structured artifact in the same repository as the retrieval system's code (not a one-off spreadsheet living in someone's personal drive that nobody remembers to update), run it automatically whenever anything that could affect retrieval quality changes -- a new embedding model, a different chunking strategy, an index parameter change -- and **turn every real production failure into a new permanent entry.**

```python
def add_regression_case(golden_dataset, query, relevant_ids):
    golden_dataset.append({"query": query, "relevant_ids": set(relevant_ids)})

golden_dataset = [{"query": "q1", "relevant_ids": {"d1"}}]

def retrieve_current(query, k):
    responses = {"q1": ["d1", "d2", "d3"], "q2": ["d5", "d6", "d7"]}
    return responses.get(query, [])[:k]

def avg_recall(dataset, retrieve_fn, k):
    scores = [
        recall_at_k(retrieve_fn(case["query"], k), case["relevant_ids"], k)
        for case in dataset
    ]
    return sum(scores) / len(scores)

print(round(avg_recall(golden_dataset, retrieve_current, 3), 3))   # 1.0 -- looks perfect so far

# A user reports that searching "q2" should have surfaced "d4", but didn't.
add_regression_case(golden_dataset, "q2", {"d4"})

print(round(avg_recall(golden_dataset, retrieve_current, 3), 3))   # drops -- now correctly reflects the known gap
```

This is exactly the 'every bug becomes a regression test' pattern that's standard practice in software testing generally, applied specifically to retrieval quality: the golden dataset's average recall looked perfect *only because it had never been asked about the specific case that was actually failing*. The moment that known failure gets added as a permanent case, the metric correctly stops overstating how well the system is doing, and stays that way for every future run -- including catching the exact moment, later, when a genuine fix finally makes that case pass again.

